;;; Under `(absent …)` the dead pattern `(says (and (paid ?g)))` holds, and the rule fires —
;;; M1f **W83**, register row `W83/pattern-argument-and-under-absent`, filed by S1f.7.3 (T1f.7.3.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, the fact `(says (paid Ann))`, and a rule that welcomes a guest nobody
;;; says has paid, `(absent (says (and (paid ?g))))`. No candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Refused at load, at the pattern, one line, on every command. With `(absent (says
;;; (paid ?g)))` the rule derives `(welcome Bob)` alone.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `52e05fc1`, release: the program loads; the inner pattern matches nothing, so the
;;; `(absent …)` holds of every guest and the rule derives `(welcome Ann)`, of whom it
;;; is said she paid, and `(welcome Bob)`, exit 0 on every command.
;;; Refused at load since S1f.7.3 (T1f.7.3.3).

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(relation says Claim :why "it is said that {?1}")
(relation welcome Guest :why "{?1} is welcome")
(says (paid Ann))
(rule greet () :match (and (is-a ?g Guest) (absent (says (and (paid ?g))))) :assert (welcome ?g) :why "{?g} is welcome: nobody says they paid")
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
(query :goal (welcome ?g))
